<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Obernai</h1><p class="st-meta">1283 CE – 1679 CE (approx.) · capital Obernai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alsatian wine town below Mont Sainte-Odile, birthplace of Alsace's patron saint and a member of the Decapolis league of imperial cities.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Obernai lies at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-sainte-odile">Mont Sainte-Odile</a> and is held to be the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-odile">Saint Odile</a>, patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> town, it was recognised as an imperial city in the 1280s, around 1283, and in 1354 it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, the league of ten Alsatian imperial cities.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> ruled this prosperous small town, which lived from wine, grain and crafts. Its market square, town hall and corn hall reflect late medieval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> prosperity. Obernai remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Like the other <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a> cities, it suffered heavily during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years War.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, French troops occupied the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1679 confirmed French rule. Obernai is now a town in Alsace, France.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1283 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.23%;width:7.88%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1283 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Obernai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alsatian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Obernai, Oberehnheim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1481&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-obernai" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Obernai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 484 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Obernai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5174 740L5918 1245"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5918 1245h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5174 740h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5174 740h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Obernai. <a href="/atlas?year=1481&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-obernai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

